      "prompt_tokens": 85,
      "completion_tokens": 702,
      "output": "**Water (H₂O)** is a simple yet remarkably versatile chemical compound that is essential for life and the planet’s climate. Here’s a concise breakdown of what makes water special:\n\n| Aspect | What it means |\n|--------|---------------|\n| **Chemical identity** | Two hydrogen atoms covalently bonded to one oxygen atom (H₂O). |\n| **Molecular shape** | Bent (V‑shaped) with a bond angle of ~104.5°, giving it a polar structure. |\n| **Polarity** | The oxygen atom carries a partial negative charge, while the hydrogens carry partial positive charges. This polarity allows water to dissolve many substances (the “universal solvent”). |\n| **Hydrogen bonding** | Each water molecule can form up to four hydrogen bonds (two as donors, two as acceptors). These bonds give water its high surface tension, cohesion, and adhesion. |\n| **States of matter** | Solid (ice), liquid (water), and gas (vapor). The transitions are governed by temperature and pressure. |\n| **Physical properties** | • Density: 1 g cm⁻³ at 4 °C (maximum). <br>• Specific heat: 4.18 kJ kg⁻¹ K⁻¹ (high, moderates climate). <br>• Surface tension: ~72 mN m⁻¹ at 20 °C. <br>• Vapor pressure: 2.3 kPa at 20 °C. |\n| **Biological role** | • Solvent for metabolic reactions. <br>• Medium for transport of nutrients and waste. <br>• Regulates temperature via sweating, respiration, and circulation. |\n| **Environmental role** | • Drives the hydrologic cycle (evaporation, condensation, precipitation). <br>• Influences weather patterns and climate. <br>• Supports ecosystems from oceans to soil. |\n| **Common uses** | • Drinking, cooking, sanitation. <br>• Industrial processes (cooling, chemical synthesis). <br>• Energy (hydropower, fuel cells). |\n\n### Why water is so important\n\n1. **Chemical versatility** – Its polarity and ability to form hydrogen bonds make it an excellent solvent for salts, sugars, acids, and many organic molecules.\n2. **Thermal properties** – High specific heat and latent heat of fusion/evaporation allow water to moderate temperature swings in organisms and the environment.\n3. **Structural role** – In cells, water provides the medium for macromolecular folding and interactions; in ice, the open lattice structure supports life in cold habitats.\n4. **Transport medium** – In plants, water carries minerals from roots to leaves; in animals, it circulates blood and lymph.\n\nIn short, water is a simple H₂O molecule whose unique physical and chemical properties make it the backbone of life, the regulator of climate, and a cornerstone of countless natural and human-made systems."
